# Line endings are pinned to LF on every platform, including Windows.
#
# scripts/verify_app_bundle.py compares the text assets bundled into the packaged app
# against the repository's site/ byte for byte. The Windows installer is built on a
# Windows runner, where Git's default autocrlf setting rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout,
# so the Windows app.asar carried CRLF copies of all 14 site/ text assets and the bundle
# check failed on a bundle that was not stale at all (launcher-v1.0.2 run 38835252575:
# site/index.html 46 CRLF, desktop.js 311, data/world.json 2134, and the size difference
# equalled the CRLF count exactly, while the Linux bundle matched).
#
# `eol` in .gitattributes takes precedence over core.autocrlf, so these rules make a
# checkout on any platform produce the same bytes the index holds. The index already
# stores every one of these as LF (`git ls-files --eol` reports no i/crlf), so this adds
# no renormalisation churn.
#
# The suffixes are exactly the ones verify_app_bundle.py compares; binary assets it
# deliberately skips (.png imagery, the third-party .jar) are left alone.
*.html        text eol=lf
*.js          text eol=lf
*.mjs         text eol=lf
*.css         text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.svg         text eol=lf
*.txt         text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.webmanifest text eol=lf
.nojekyll     text eol=lf

# The release pipeline itself is read by bash on the Linux runner and by Node on the
# Windows runner; keep both reading the same bytes.
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
*.cjs         text eol=lf
